\begin_layout Section
Conclusions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Frame
\begin_inset Argument 4
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Main Conclusions and limitations
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The GVAR methodology proved a parsimonious modeling of the labor market
 at regional level, (mesoregion) taking into account the external influences
 of each region.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The model showed the dynamic relationship between the mesoregions, as well
 as it was possible to determine the elasticity of the regions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The South, Southeast and Northeast have shown to be very elastic, presenting
 a constant interaction in their regions, while the North and Central regions
 seem to suffer little influence from the neighboring regions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Limitations 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Small sample of employment series, data availiable only from 2004 onwards.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The GVAR toolbox does not currently incorporate dummy variables, or seasonality
 analysis.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Extensions Evaluation of the model taking into account the American industrial
 production.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Section
Possible Extensions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Frame
\begin_inset Argument 4
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Assets and Liabilities returns:
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
A more comprehensive macroeconomic model can be formulated and different
 types of macroeconomic shocks can be identified and their impact simulated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
It is possible to construct a “tailor-made” GVAR.
 This route was explored by Ericsson and Reisman [2012] but not in a regional
 model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Disagregate data by not only regions but also sectors such as agriculture,
 industry and services among others.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Another possible path of research consists of evaluating predictive performance
 at different aggregate levels and horizons against simple benchmarks such
 as random walk or first order autoregressive model.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
